# Slot 4 (Calibrated Confidence, Economics, deficiency, standard) — review log

## 2026-04-10 cycle 10 — fact pack curation

**Fact pack written:** `corpus/fact-packs/09-economics-call-center-bonus-field-experiment-01.md`

**Scenario design rationale:** A call-center field experiment with 420 agents, 8-week randomized comparison of a 5%-of-pay productivity bonus vs control. The main effect (7% productivity increase, 95% CI 12–32 calls per week, p < 0.01, N=420, pre-registered) is strongly supported — exactly the kind of result that deserves confident language. The two subgroup analyses (tenure, baseline productivity) are weaker: one is not significant (p=0.16), the other is marginally significant at p=0.04 but comes from an uncorrected set of two subgroup tests. Long-term effects are entirely unknown because of the 8-week window. This gives the virtuous reasoner a clean differentiated-confidence opportunity — strong on the main effect, weak on the subgroups, explicit unknown on the long-term.

The **deficiency failure mode** for this slot is the reasoner applying flat weak hedging to the main effect as well as the subgroup claims, treating the well-supported 7% result as if it were just suggestive rather than robust. The failure is not that the subgroups are hedged (they should be) — it is that the main effect is hedged at the same level.

